module bindings.java.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders;

// The Java twin of bindings.javascript.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders and
// bindings.python.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders: a client that follows a 3xx
// itself decides what the next request carries, and the part that cannot simply
// be copied is the credential set -- `Authorization` and `Cookie` first among
// them -- because the 3xx is written by the server the first request reached
// and names the next host, so a copy of the header set hands that server's
// operator whatever bearer token, session cookie or basic-auth credential the
// caller attached for the original host. That is CWE-200 with no injection
// anywhere in it: the URL the client fetches is exactly the one it was told to
// fetch.
//
// The JavaScript and Python twins read three facts from the follower's own body:
// a `Location` read, the header store being copied, and a redirect budget. A
// Java client of any size does not keep those in one method -- the follower
// reads the Location and the budget, then hands the previous request's headers
// to a helper that builds the propagated set for the next hop. The helper is
// where the credential strip lives and where a fix lands, so the helper is what
// this binding reads. Its own body carries the same three facts in a helper's
// spelling: a hop rewrite -- removing `Host` or the content framing
// (`Content-Length`, `Content-Type`), which a re-sent request must drop or
// rewrite because the target and the body change -- is what makes the store a
// propagated header set rather than an incidental map; a credential strip --
// removing `Authorization` or `Proxy-Authorization` -- is what makes the
// function a credential-boundary handler rather than a framing fixer; and the
// absence of a `Cookie` removal is the leak, because the HTTP specifications
// and the WHATWG HTTP-redirect fetch both put Cookie in the credential set a
// change of origin has to drop, and every mainstream client that strips
// Authorization strips Cookie in the same breath. A helper that rewrote framing
// and kept every credential names no credential header at all, so requiring the
// Authorization removal keeps this on the partial strip -- the follower that
// considered the boundary and dropped half of it.
//
// The three facts are read off call-argument tokens, `remove:Host` and the
// like, which pair each header name with the removal method it was passed to:
// the paired form is what separates a `Cookie` *removal* from every other way a
// redirect handler touches cookies -- re-adding them from a URI-scoped cookie
// store, iterating them, naming a `CookieStore` field -- all of which name
// cookies freely and would silence a negation read over bare argument or
// identifier text. Header names are fixed by the HTTP specifications, so the
// needles are answerable to those specifications and not to any one client's
// vocabulary: netty's `HttpHeaders.remove(CharSequence)`, a `Map` keyed by
// header names, and quoted spellings of the names all lower-case to the same
// tokens.
//
// Residual, both directions. A strip spelled through a different removal
// method -- Apache HttpClient's `removeHeader`, okhttp's `removeAll` -- is not
// read, because the token pairs the name with its own callee; the paired form
// that makes the negation precise prices the positive clauses the same way. And
// a removal whose argument merely *contains* a needle suppresses or satisfies
// it: `remove(COOKIE_JAR)` reads as a cookie removal, so an unrelated
// same-prefixed key silences the finding -- the false-negative direction only.

binding javaRedirectStripKeepsCookie {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["remove:HOST", "remove:CONTENT_LENGTH", "remove:CONTENT_TYPE"])
    and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["remove:AUTHORIZATION", "remove:PROXY_AUTHORIZATION"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["remove:COOKIE"])
  emit issue code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect at node
}
